  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1054 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nTonight through Tuesday...\n\nA line of storms swept through the region this evening, bringing \ndamaging winds, and a potential tornado near Avoca, NE.  \n\nA 50 kt LLJ will nose into the region tonight, bringing a resurgence \nof showers and storms to the region. Observed PWATs around 2\" exceed \nthe 99th percentile, climatologically. Rainfall rates of 2\" per hour \nwill be possible with deep warm cloud layers of around 10,000 ft. \nCAM QPF totals suggest rainfall amounts of 1-3\" along and south of a \nline from Lincoln to Omaha, with higher amounts, up to 3-5\" in far \nsouthwest Iowa. Areas of flash flooding appear likely through \nTuesday morning, as several storms likely train over the same \nlocations. A Flash Flood Watch remains in effect for most of \nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa through Tuesday evening, \nwhen the majority of precip finally shifts south of the forecast\narea. \n\nWednesday and Beyond...\n\nA steady stream of shortwave energy will continue \nWednesday into Thursday while the surface boundary slowly meanders \nback north, eventually reaching SD by Friday. At least on and off \nshowers and storms will be possible through that period, with \npotential for repeated rounds of rain to lead to flooding concerns. \nFriday is generally favored to be a drying period, though still \nlooking at 20- 30 percent chance of rain in the afternoon with a few \nvarious ensemble members a little slower with the northward \nadvancement of the front. However, any dry period looks to be short-\nlived as a cold front pushes in from the northwest for the weekend \nand gives us continued 30-50% chances of rain. Machine learning \nsevere weather guidance keeps severe weather potential below (under \n5%) through this active period, though again, potential heavy rain \nand flooding will remain a concern with each successive round.\n\nOtherwise, temperatures may be a bit of a roller coaster through the \nweek and will hinge on precipitation on a given day.\nCurrently, upper 60s and 70s are favored Tuesday and Wednesday \nbefore the front moves back north and marks a return to the 80s \nThursday and Friday. Behind the weekend cold front, expect to be \nback down in the 70s to lower 80s. But again, lots of moving parts, \nso confidence in exact temperatures on a given day is relatively low.",
